.TH CLEVO-CC 1 "2026-10-11" "clevo-cc-linux 0.2.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
clevo-cc \- command-line client for Clevo fan, performance and battery controls
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B clevo-cc
[\fB\-\-transport\fR \fImock|driver|acpi-call|dbus\fR]
[\fB\-\-fixture\fR \fIFIle\fR]
\fISubcommand\fR [\fIOptions\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B clevo-cc
reads fan status, fan curves, capabilities and power information.
It is dry-run by default:
every write needs
.B \-\-apply
and, when talking to
.BR clevod ,
a PolicyKit authorization.
.SH TRANSPORTS
.TP
.B mock
(default)
Replay a fixture; never touches hardware.
.TP
.B acpi-call
Read-only, via
.IR /proc/acpi/call .
Rejects every write before any I/O.
.TP
.B driver
Read the clevo-cc kernel driver's sysfs/hwmon interface directly.
Direct writes require appropriate file permissions.
Charging-limit writes require the dbus transport.
.TP
.B dbus
Talk to
.BR clevod
over
.BR org.clevo.CC ;
reads need no privilege, writes go through PolicyKit.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
clevo\-cc \-\-transport dbus doctor
clevo\-cc \-\-transport driver doctor \-\-json
clevo\-cc \-\-fixture fixtures/example.fixture fan status
sudo clevo\-cc \-\-transport acpi\-call fan watch \-\-interval\-ms 1000
clevo\-cc \-\-transport dbus fan status
clevo\-cc \-\-transport dbus fan set\-mode max \-\-apply
clevo\-cc \-\-transport dbus fan set\-curve \-\-cpu "40,20 60,40 80,70 100,100" \-\-apply
clevo\-cc \-\-transport dbus power status \-\-json
clevo\-cc \-\-transport dbus power set\-thresholds BAT0 \-\-start 40 \-\-end 80 \-\-enabled true
clevo\-cc \-\-transport dbus power set\-thresholds BAT0 \-\-start 40 \-\-end 80 \-\-enabled true \-\-apply
.fi
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B doctor
prints a read-only, best-effort report covering the kernel, ACPI/DMI, driver,
keyboard, power interfaces, permissions, D-Bus service and daemon configuration.
Individual probe failures do not stop the report. It does not activate the
daemon, call Poll, change hardware settings or rewrite configuration.
.PP
Use
.B \-\-json
for structured output and
.B \-\-config PATH
to validate the daemon's configuration (default: /etc/clevo-cc/clevod.toml).
The mock backend only validates the offline fixture and does not probe the host.
Select driver, acpi-call or dbus explicitly to diagnose the installed system.
.PP
Statuses are Pass, Warn, Fail and Skip. Optional unsupported interfaces are
skipped; unreadable evidence is reported separately from a confirmed fault.
Doctor exits 1 if any check fails, or 0 when only passes, warnings and skips remain.
.PP
The acpi-call backend sends read-only queries through /proc/acpi/call;
this writes query requests to the interface but never sends settings commands.
.PP
Doctor checks standard charging nodes and legacy platform-profile paths. Their
absence does not rule out native FlexiCharger or a modern platform-profile
provider. Use
.B \-\-transport dbus power status \-\-json
to inspect the daemon's detected backend; doctor does not test charging writes.
.SH POWER
.B power status
reads Linux battery and platform-profile interfaces, including native
FlexiCharger when available. Add
.B \-\-json
for structured output. Missing measurements remain unknown.
.PP
.B \-\-transport dbus power set\-thresholds BAT0 \-\-end 80
validates a charging limit without writing. Add
.B \-\-start 40
only when the battery supports a start threshold, and
.B \-\-apply
to commit through the dbus transport and PolicyKit. Omitting start preserves
the existing start threshold. Require 0 <= start < end <= 100.
For an existing 95/100 window, lowering end to 80 also requires lowering start.
Native FlexiCharger uses firmware-advertised discrete percentages; add
.B \-\-enabled true
or
.B \-\-enabled false
to enable or disable its charge window. Standard threshold drivers do not
accept this option. Disabling preserves the selected window.
The daemon verifies readback, attempts restoration on failure and persists
successful settings for startup replay. Unsupported hardware is refused.
Setting an end limit does not discharge a battery already above it. Kernel
Not charging and UPower fully-charged can both describe a battery drawing no
charging current; a plugged-in desktop icon alone is not a charging measurement.
.PP
The kernel platform-profile provider maps quiet/low-power/balanced/performance
to vendor modes 0/1/3/2 respectively. The daemon observes desktop changes and
persists them. Initial mode is unknown until a successful write or saved-mode
restore; registering the provider does not change the hardware mode.
.SH FAN CURVES
.B fan set\-curve
writes a four\-point curve (command 14) and selects the
.B custom
fan mode, which is what makes the firmware use it.
Points are given as
.IR "temp,duty" ,
with duty in percent; temperatures must strictly increase.
.B \-\-gpu1
defaults to the CPU curve and
.B \-\-gpu2
to an all\-zero curve, which leaves that channel alone.
.PP
Command 14 replaces the whole table, so the stack reads the current curve first
and merges only the channels you name; channels you omit keep their values.
.PP
Selecting the custom curve is a separate step in the driver interface, but
.B set\-curve
does it for you: writing a curve without selecting
.B custom
would leave the firmware on its auto curve.
.PP
Only the middle two points of each fan reach the EC; it keeps its own first and
last point. All four are still required so the curve you send is a complete
description, and the two that are not written are accepted as given.
.PP
A successful write is persisted by
.BR clevod (8)
to
.IR /etc/clevo\-cc/clevod.toml
and replayed on the next daemon start, so a custom curve survives a reboot. The
EC forgets it on power loss; the daemon re-sends command 14 and then selects
.BR custom .
.SH SAFETY
Writes need
.B \-\-apply
and, over D\-Bus, a PolicyKit authorization; without an authentication agent
the daemon denies non\-root callers rather than silently allowing them.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR clevod (8)
